WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.180
This person's an airline pilot, so they're gone quite a bit.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.580
Paul Jareffi is a letter carrier in Fort Lauderdale.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.140
He's been doing the same route on and off for 15 years.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.060
This house next door here, one of the original Miami Dolphins lives there from the 1966 team.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:28.300
So this guy here just passed away about a year ago.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.320
He was a retired mail carrier.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.020
He was a really nice old guy.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:37.240
Hey.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:39.700
What's going on?

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:40.940
I was just talking about your father.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
It's not just that the mailman knows a little bit about every person in the neighborhood.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.360
The mailman also talks to people.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.300
Who else in your neighborhood has talked to as many of your neighbors?

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.380
And because of all that, letter carriers just get into situations.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:04.180
One day, Paul was driving in his mail truck, And he saw these two guys on the sidewalk in front of an office building near the entrance to the parking lot.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.520
And they were punching each other and fighting and wrestling.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:12.460
And as I'm coming closer, I noticed some people standing there watching.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.660
One guy, I remember, clearly had his arms folded.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.880
And I'm thinking, OK, they must be filming a movie.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.580
Sometimes they film movies in the area.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.000
And that was my initial thought.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.520
Well, I kind of recognized this guy from delivering his mail occasionally.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.990
Occasionally, he owned a pizza place there.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:36.900
So as I got closer, he saw the mail truck and he started waving his hand back and forth for me to stop.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:47.600
So I slowed down and he had this guy, you know, by the pants with one arm and he put his hand to his ear to like for me to call for help.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.830
And he's saying, help, help.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:54.280
So Paul pulls over and he calls 911 when he notices that the pizza shop owner must have been stabbed.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.440
There were these huge circles of blood on his clothes.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.500
The two guys are still tussling on the ground.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:05.280
So in the midst of this, what really struck me as odd is a person that worked at the office building is pulling into the parking lot.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:06.820
These two people are fighting.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.920
You know, a guy saying, let me go, let me go, leave me alone.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:21.660
And she very carefully steers around them, goes into the parking lot, parks her car, gets out, looks back for a second, goes into the office building like, well, I've got work to do or something.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.920
So I heard the sirens coming.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:32.320
I'm pleading with the dispatcher, hurry, hurry.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.640
Then the police came, and an officer got out and cuffed the guy right away.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.660
And ironically, the guy that had been stabbed ran.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.100
He ran away back to his restaurant because he was worried that he had left the door unlocked.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.320
So they had to catch him and sit him down until the ambulance got there.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:03:00.200
Paul found out later that what had happened was the pizza shop owner had fired the younger guy, told him to get out.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.940
The guy responded by stabbing the shop owner five times.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:13.840
But when he tried to get away, the shop owner then chased the guy out of the shop and maybe 50 yards to the back of the parking lot, which is where he caught him and where Paul first saw them.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:23.440
Paul was credited with saving the shop owner's life, though he went back to the building after all this happened, looking to talk to anybody else who'd been there and seen it.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.600
Because he really did not understand.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:33.740
What struck me really odd about the whole situation was that so many people stood there and did nothing.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.120
And I talked to one of the guys later that was a maintenance man in the building.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.000
I said, you know, how could you just stood there?

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.600
And he goes, well, I don't know what was going on.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.220
I didn't know who did what to who.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:44.980
I didn't want to get involved.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:46.840
And that's what the gentleman said.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:47.920
You know, people drove by.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.880
They were standing there, and nobody helped me until the mailman came by.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:07.080
The National Association of Letter Carriers named Paul one of its Heroes of the Year in 2008 for this.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:08.840
Others who were named that year?

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.840
A postal carrier in Cedar Rapids who crawled inside a partially submerged car and rescued the driver.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.340
A carrier in New York who alerted authorities to what became a major identity theft case.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:24.120
I've been noticing tons of credit card mail in the name of an 85-year-old woman who'd been in a nursing home for months.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:33.800
It's so common for letter carriers to stumble onto something during their route and perform a good deed that these incidents are a regular feature of the Letter Carrier Association's monthly magazine.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.680
These postal workers, they see what is happening around them, and they feel responsible.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.120
Versus the rest of us.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.430
We're more like the people standing on the sidewalk.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.320
And today's show is about the tension between those two different ways of reacting to trouble.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.320
Which kind of person do we want to be?

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:51.220
I mean, really.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:52.020
Not pretending.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:52.700
Really.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:05:00.520
Our show today on 4X, 4X where there are decent reasons for people to step in and help, and even better reasons not to.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:02.160
Stay with us.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.440
Equine, wary home companions.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.130
We got the idea for today's program when we heard about this woman named Emily Feldman.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:27.500
Emily Feldman lived on the same block in the same house for 40 years before she decided to turn to her neighbors for help.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:35.120
And the way that she turned to her neighbors for help raises the question, just how much can we reasonably expect from our neighbors?

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:40.120
Some background, Emily lives in a small middle-class suburb called Fairlawn, New Jersey.

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.820
Her son Scott has autism.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:42.340
He's 39.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:47.560
Because he's 39, this has put Emily into a situation that a lot of parents like her get into.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:47.720
to.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:52.520
She's a widow, and though she is a robust 67 years old, she is getting older.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.460
And so she needs to think about what is going to happen to Scott, who's going to look after him when she's gone.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:04.480
The solution she came up with to that problem is ambitious, and it is going to be really, really difficult to pull off.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:06.520
Ruth Paddler tells the story.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:11.420
Emily's plan actually has two parts, and one of them is a real long shot.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:15.080
She's trying to recruit volunteers from all over Fairlawn.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:24.680
Neighbors, strangers, anyone who's willing to sign up to be Scott's friend, to serve collectively as Scott's surrogate mom after Emily herself is gone.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.260
But first she had to lay the foundation.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:34.320
She'd already made sure Scott knew how to drive and cook and shop for his own food, but at 38 he was still living at home.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:39.400
So she bought Scott a house, basically so he could have a trial run at living on his own.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:50.780
I wanted to be there for the mistakes because I felt that by the time, you know, something happens to me, he would know that he's fine.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.420
I mean, I'm here.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:59.520
I can make sure it's going right and, you know, make those corrections that nobody will have that, quote, burden.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:02.680
I don't want anyone to have that responsibility.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.540
So this way, I'll see it.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:09.740
Emily couldn't have a better vantage point.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.640
Scott's house is smack across the street from hers.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:17.420
It's a cute Cape Cod with essentially the same floor plan as the house where he grew up.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.160
Except this one's his.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:24.920
On the summer day when Scott gave me a tour of his new place, he'd lived there only a few days.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:28.740
This is the football pillow I got from Walgreens for myself.

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.360
And this ladybug pillow I got from Bed Bath &amp; Beyond.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:38.580
I was going to use it for my bedroom, but it looks better on this sofa here.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.720
The house became available last spring, after the previous owner died.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:50.340
Emily is a schoolteacher on a limited budget, but she snagged a 1% mortgage for Scott through a state program for disabled adults.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.000
He makes the payments with his Social Security income.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:56.720
The house is very tidy, but the setup is kind of strange.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:01.580
It looks like what a 10-year-old boy might come up with if he had the chance to live on his own.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.740
There's a dolphin nightlight in the bedroom, a list on the wall of U.S.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:12.380
presidents' birthdays and birthplaces that he got off the Internet, and tons of Disney DVDs and stuffed animals throughout the house.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:19.760
In the kitchen, Scott's taped hooks on the outside of each cabinet door and then hung a cooking utensil on each one.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:28.140
It's practical, but it looks a bit surreal, as if the ladles and spatulas simply floated out of the drawers and got stuck on their way to the ceiling.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:33.680
It's easier for me to know what's there when I need it, and I don't have to mix it up in the drawers.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:39.680
Not surprisingly, Scott's had a few mishaps since he moved in.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:47.540
Early on, he ripped out the metal tracking for all the closet doors because he didn't know what they were for, and then dumped them at the recycling depot.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:54.060
And last month, he strung an extension cord across the living room floor, then threw a blanket over it to cover it up.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.540
It took a while for Emily to convince him what a bad idea it was and that he should just tuck it under the carpet.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:08:59.700
bit.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:05.440
It's as if now that Scott's in his own place, every day is a dress rehearsal for Emily's death.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.200
Another day where she has a glimpse of how Scott will live without her.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.260
Emily thought that once Scott got into his own house, the hard part would be over.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:26.620
But Scott has a tendency to isolate himself, and Emily worries the house gives him the perfect cave to disappear into.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:35.440
She's afraid that once she's gone, he'll withdraw, spend days without talking with anyone, and wind up feeling even more depressed, which he's done in the past.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:42.560
So Emily wants to recruit people to check in on him, help him solve problems, take him out for a game of ping pong.

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:55.240
She could use state funds to hire a caretaker or place him in a group home, but Scott's always hated following someone else's rules, so much so that he used to hurl the TV and and yanked the toilet seats off their hinges when he felt boxed in.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:57.560
I don't like people telling me what to do.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:04.300
It's like people just like to steal my rights by annoying me with commands and threats.

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:11.960
Emily spent the whole summer working diligently for Scott, trying to find volunteers who could create his safety net for after she's gone.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:21.400
She wants volunteers instead of paid helpers, not just because of the expense, but because she believes volunteers will be devoted to Scott, not a paycheck.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:28.460
She's not expecting anyone to be his mom, but she is hoping that if enough people step forward, Scott will be okay.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:33.140
By the time I'm not here, I want to know that people are there for him in some way.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.040
And they don't have to do the work.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:41.020
It's just look in, and if you see something's not right, you know, there are other people to call.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:42.220
You don't have to do anything.

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:43.780
You don't have to spend any money.

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:46.740
You know, it's just being there, you know?

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:49.440
I can't plan out everything.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:50.280
I'm not stupid.

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:50.480
bit.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.420
But what I can plan out, I want to.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:11.260
All right, I made this flyer to put up in places with, if you notice, the letters are very big.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.000
Big brother volunteer wanted.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:20.220
And then high functioning young man needs a friend to accompany him to movies or bowling, the recreation center?

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:25.880
A week earlier, Emily had stopped in at Benny's Luncheonette, the diner around the corner from where she and Scott live.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:30.500
She asked if she could post a flyer saying, Autistic man wants friends.

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:34.080
The owner thought the autism reference might scare people off.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:38.300
So today, Emily comes back with her new version and tapes it to the door.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:47.780
She spends the next several hours working her way through town with a grocery bag full of flyers.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:49.900
She heads into the police station.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:52.740
Is there somebody that I could speak with?

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:55.920
It's not a dangerous matter.

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:00.510
I was wondering if they have, like, a Big Brother organization or...

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:07.060
Emily makes her pitch through a thick layer of glass, leaning in to be sure the cop on the other side can hear her.

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:20.360
But I kind of was thinking, even if they don't have an organization, would there be any policemen, young or old or whatever, who would want to be sort of a friend, a big brother, look in on him, you know.

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:26.940
Nothing's coming to my head right now as far as a referral for you.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:30.560
The dispatcher gives Emily the number for the community policing officer.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.440
It's not particularly promising, but Emily is undeterred.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:36.680
Next stop, the town senior center.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:41.920
There she spins things differently, to play up what Scott has to offer, not just what he needs.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:44.920
She trots out every possible lure she can think of.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.220
She tells the director that Scott likes old people.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:52.300
He admires them because he thinks they're older and they know more, more about history.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:58.860
And they're very into presidents, and they were born at a time when there were the presidents that he never knew.

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:06.900
So to me it felt like it could do something nice for a person who maybe lost their wife or, you know, who's alone, who's sitting here.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:12.880
and now they could have somebody who maybe they don't drive and maybe they need someone to pick up a quart of milk.

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:15.360
He would do things like that, you know what I mean?

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:16.780
And now he has a house.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.920
They could come over and he could teach them some things.

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:21.740
So he's good with the computer and stuff.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:22.820
Great.

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:23.840
I'll post this.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:28.460
I worried that the director was just sending her flyer to bulletin board purgatory.

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:34.220
She could have offered to announce Emily's proposal during the daily lunch program or the all-day bingo games.

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.460
Next, Emily heads to the mayor's office.

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:40.970
She's been at this for hours, and she's clearly getting frazzled.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:53.160
I wanted to ask, I've been really trying for the longest time, and I have been trying in the hardest way possible to get, like, volunteers and people who would stop in on him.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:58.700
The two clerks behind the counter seem uncomfortable, maybe a bit embarrassed about how blatantly Emily's pleading.

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:04.200
So I just felt that, you know, because the mayor is the mayor, that he has to know people.

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:04.900
He's the mayor.

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:12.540
You know, I just feel that way about it, and I would love to see somebody sort of step up and say, you know, I'm going to try and help you.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:18.100
The clerks suggest Emily try calling the Human Services Department, or the Health Department, or the Rotary Club.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:20.640
Emily tells them she has tried those things.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:26.060
She has also tried the town's high school, the local college, and her congressman, and gotten nowhere.

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:32.500
During her 20 minutes with the clerks, it's hard to ignore her rising sense of panic about what will happen to Scott.

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:34.100
if volunteers don't come through.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:38.640
And the day comes that I'm not here, it would just be nice if people just knew who he was.

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:41.300
I don't want him to be a prisoner in his own house.

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:42.980
You know what I'm saying?

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:52.670
You know what, also through our Office of Emergency Management, they have like an emergency phone chain and all, and if someone has special...

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:19.660
a couple months after emily knocked on doors and put up flyers i checked in with her to see how things were going despite her efforts not a single person had responded to her appeals in town your plan was sort of this two-pronged plan one get him the house and two get volunteers to to sort of be the safety net underneath him.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:22.480
Obviously, the house part has worked out.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:23.040
Beautifully.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:24.880
How about the other part?

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:26.840
The other part hasn't worked out.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:35.780
It's funny, I thought about that, too, how I really had, I was all going to know that all of this would just fall into place, and I got tired of asking is what happened.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:36.740
It wore me out.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:39.680
I really didn't want to beg people.

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.260
I figured the story was good enough.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:43.600
You know what I mean?

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:46.100
Because you see it on television.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:16:18.500
you know you do you see them push the bus here's the bus i said i've never seen a bus i'm waiting for the bus to come okay well i don't get the oh you don't know that program on television emily explains she's talking about extreme makeover home edition the abc reality show where trades people aided by volunteers in the community renovate the house of a family suffering from hardship a family like emily's every episode the producers send the family on a week-long vacation, and when they return, a giant tour bus is hiding the spiffed-up house.

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:20.360
And then they go, move the bus!

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:22.320
Move the bus!

00:16:28.580 --> 00:16:34.520
And all the neighbors are there, and all the, you know, the whole neighborhood actually pitches in and helps.

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:37.720
And it's like, wow, they did this for this family.

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:38.600
Isn't this nice?

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:47.160
I mean, it really would be beautiful, because you do get tired.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.600
But I can't walk away from it, and I can't just turn my back on it either.

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:02.280
My producer Jonathan and I asked Emily why she thought the community wasn't responding the way she'd expected.

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:07.040
Could it be the people were afraid of the responsibility of having someone rely on them?

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:09.640
Or all the messy emotions that went along with that?

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:18.500
She said she didn't think so, but then she started talking about how a while back she and another teacher helped out a colleague, a 50-year-old woman dying of lung cancer.

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:25.020
They drove her to doctor's appointments, hauled her in her oxygen tank to Broadway shows, and sat with her in her last days.

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:29.240
And the hard part was I knew she was going to die.

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:33.840
I, you know, that was difficult dealing with that and being with it.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:36.380
It took a lot out of me, very honestly.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:39.760
And would I do it again so readily?

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:40.860
No.

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:45.760
I did it, and I'm glad I did it, and she had a wonderful spirit.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.900
But, God, I can't give that much of myself.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:54.020
It sounds like you do understand why people would be reluctant.

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:54.900
I do.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:55.900
I do.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:02.760
I absolutely understand because you put yourself in it emotionally, you know?

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:02.860
know?

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:07.720
So you're right about the fact that a lot of people, they're aware of that.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:11.150
You know, what if I get involved here and I don't want to be involved?

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:17.860
And so there is that, you know, people stay clear of it maybe for that reason.

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:21.800
The reality is that Scott does need more than just an occasional pal.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:30.060
Nearly every night for years, he's gone to Emily, wanting to talk about pain from the past, like being taunted and rejected in high school.

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:32.660
Why didn't people people understand, he asks.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:33.900
Why am I autistic?

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:37.060
See, I'm not happy about what I'm born with.

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:39.040
I claim that remark.

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:48.540
And because I was born with autism, I usually have a lot of fear and fright about life.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:53.680
Even today, I get a little scared of people sometimes.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:58.680
It's that fear of socializing that sabotaged the one nibble of interest that Emily got.

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:04.300
She had placed an ad on Craigslist seeking a volunteer to work with a high-functioning autistic man.

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:12.560
Only one person answered Emily's ad, a 62-year-old unemployed construction worker from the Philippines named Pru Dumlao.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:14.240
I was looking for a job.

00:19:14.300 --> 00:19:15.420
It's very hard to find a job.

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:18.540
So I have to do something.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.100
And then I saw him.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:21.720
And I think this one's good.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:27.840
Pru called a lot at first, and his enthusiasm made Emily uneasy, suspicious even.

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:31.480
She wondered why on earth a pure stranger would want to help out Scott.

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:33.420
This was an odd turn of events.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:37.660
Here she was getting exactly what she'd wanted, and it creeped her out.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:42.800
Eventually, Emily realized Prue had no family here and was probably just lonely and bored.

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:45.220
She ended up inviting him to hang out with Scott.

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:48.020
Hello, how are you?

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:49.260
Come in, come in.

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:51.040
Scott was waiting for you.

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:54.940
Emily invited Prue to go to the town recreation center with Scott.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.640
By then, Prue had landed a full-time job.

00:19:57.940 --> 00:19:59.780
But he agreed to join Scott just the same.

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:03.560
Scott drove Prue and me to the rec center, super cautiously.

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:06.980
Prue sat in the passenger seat, looking totally at ease.

00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:13.260
Scott rambled on about fire and firefighters, and it was really nice the way Prue tried to engage him.

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:14.660
He stuck with Scott.

00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:16.160
He didn't talk down to him.

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:18.640
A fire chief can give you complete information.

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:20.320
Did you meet a fire chief here?

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:23.520
There's a fire chief on...

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:24.660
You met him already?

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:30.880
At the rec center, Scott and Prue ended up at the air hockey table.

00:20:31.900 --> 00:20:33.120
Scott got totally into it.

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:34.580
In fact, he was beaming.

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.820
By then, Emily had arrived, too, and she was stunned.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:39.840
She had almost never seen him that happy.

00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:41.920
I got you good.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:44.220
I got you good.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:47.680
Prue and Scott kept laughing every time they scored.

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:54.140
But 20 minutes after they arrived, smack in the midst of the action, Scott abruptly announced he wanted to go home.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:55.120
And left.

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:02.860
Emily and Prue haven't talked since that day at the rec center.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.000
The experience embarrassed Emily.

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:10.120
Here she was, begging people to spend time with Scott, and then his autism interfered.

00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:13.800
It makes him afraid of spending too much time with people he doesn't know well.

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:18.060
It's another roadblock, but for now, maybe it doesn't matter.

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:24.180
Scott's been doing more and more on his own, and he's actually reaching out to neighbors for help, all without Emily.

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:29.180
Just having the house has changed him, made him more confident, and even more sociable.

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:35.940
A few weeks ago, when he was out raking leaves, he knocked on the door of his 84-year-old neighbor and offered to rake her yard, too.

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:41.840
He asked another neighbor, a guy he barely knew, to help him haul a table, and the guy said sure.

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:45.640
He started to really rely on a man who lives across the street.

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:52.860
A bunch of times, Scott's even sat on the guy's front steps late at night, chatting with him about real stuff, like Scott's frustrations.

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:59.420
And the other day, when the guy heard Emily was going out of town for a few days, he offered to look in on Scott.

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:01.400
Emily didn't even need to ask.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:04.400
It's not the bus, but it's a start.

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:15.280
Ruth Padua writes for the New York Times Magazine.

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:21.800
Act Two, Baby Steps.

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:24.960
You know, sometimes you are the problem.

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:29.520
You know, you are the event that everyone in your block, everyone on the street has to decide.

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:31.080
Are they going to say something to you?

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:31.980
Are they going to step in?

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:34.680
This happened to Ryan Knighton.

00:22:35.940 --> 00:22:40.400
He became the thing that everyone on the street had to react to for reasons that will become clear enough.

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:45.820
Ryan is Canadian, and I only point that out because in the course of his story, he uses the word nappy.

00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:49.040
This was originally written and produced for Canadian radio.

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:53.720
I think I'll take Tess for a walk, I announced one morning.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:55.680
Tess is our four-month-old.

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:58.100
This would be our first walk together.

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:00.760
My wife Tracy paused.

00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:03.160
Oh, she said.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:05.360
Uh, okay.

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:12.780
I've already got the baby chest sack carrier thing, I said, if you can put her sweater on or whatever.

00:23:14.180 --> 00:23:15.960
You, uh, you sure?

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:17.180
Tracy asked.

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:17.280
asked.

00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:20.580
I was already busy putting the harness on inside out.

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.040
Yep, we'll be fine.

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:26.180
I'll go with you then, Tracy said.

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:28.640
She sounded extra cheerful, persuasive.

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:35.200
I couldn't angle a kind way to say, but I don't want you to walk with us.

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:39.020
For once, I want to take Tess for a walk by myself.

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:44.180
So instead, I said, look, you never get any time alone.

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:46.860
Tracy didn't need reminding.

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:52.500
In four months, she'd never been further than the shower without Tess either in her arms or in sight.

00:23:53.200 --> 00:23:54.960
She hadn't much in the way of alternatives either.

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:58.440
I'm her husband, but I'm also blind.

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:03.100
And people are naturally wary of leaving me in charge of a baby.

00:24:03.970 --> 00:24:04.780
Even my own.

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:15.660
Though I've been blind for ten years now, I'm still not very good at it.

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:24.640
I lost my sight slowly, over a 15-year stretch, a slow and painless deterioration of my retinas caused by a genetic misfire with a long name.

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:31.980
While I've adapted to much over time, four months with a baby is a slender window in which to perfect my new dad skills.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:35.700
Just imagine changing a nappy in the dark.

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:39.620
What was once a diaper is now psychedelic origami.

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:45.300
Today my plan was to strap on our child for a walk through city traffic.

00:24:46.280 --> 00:24:48.000
You can understand Tracy's reluctance.

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:51.020
It's just a little walk, I said.

00:24:51.760 --> 00:24:52.210
We'll be fine.

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:57.400
You have the baby carrier on upside down, she said, surrendering.

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:04.620
A few minutes later, I descended our front stoop with Tess.

00:25:05.620 --> 00:25:08.820
I have never been more petrified as a blind pedestrian.

00:25:09.860 --> 00:25:17.760
Tess was harnessed to my belly, and the weight of her there, there, that new presence against my chest and stomach brought other sensations to the surface.

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:22.780
I could feel memories of mushing my gut into any number of undetected obstacles.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:28.520
Into poles, bicycles, parking meters, chain link fences, you name it.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:34.480
I stepped cautiously, deliberately, as if carrying a sack of sweaty dynamite.

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:37.360
I swept my cane with the care of a mind detector.

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:40.580
Twenty minutes later, we'd only made it to the corner.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:43.480
We live two doors down from the corner.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:51.920
The first person to pass us saw the situation in simpler terms.

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:55.620
Jesus, that's got to be tricky, she said as she passed.

00:25:56.340 --> 00:26:03.580
Maybe she said it to me, or maybe to a person she was walking with, or maybe, as her phrasing suggested, to her pal Jesus.

00:26:04.580 --> 00:26:07.100
Already strangers were praying for our survival.

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:21.040
Within the next block and the next 20 minutes of slow-going movement, at least a half a dozen others offered similar prayers, or insisted on guiding us, or asked to take us home, or asked if we'd lost mommy.

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:35.380
Slowly, we edged around the corner at Grant Street and left the residential sidewalks for commercial drive.

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:38.980
More people meant more noise to govern by.

00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:40.200
A good thing.

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:45.700
The sound of traffic stretched into the distance, so at least I had something pointing me in the right direction.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:48.640
The help of crowds has a backhand, though.

00:26:49.460 --> 00:26:51.580
Busy people pay less attention to their surroundings.

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:56.420
Folks regularly clip my shoulders, and I've been caught off balance and knocked down before.

00:26:57.380 --> 00:27:02.520
Here they might even slam us head-on, or smush a slice of hot, cheap pizza into Tess's face.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:04.780
And there would be dogs, too.

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:06.820
Usually it's pitbulls around here.

00:27:06.820 --> 00:27:13.860
Here, pit bulls leashed to bike racks or snoozing in front of doorways, as you'd find them in their native habitat by the gates of Hades.

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:22.380
Too often I've whacked my cane against a dog where no dog should be, and too many times large, toothy shadows have snapped at my legs.

00:27:23.600 --> 00:27:25.380
Tess could become a chew toy.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:35.680
I waved my free hand in front of us, braced my arm and pushed ahead the way running backs rush into a dog pile, but really, really slowly.

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:38.640
Within ten steps, somebody clipped my shoulder.

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:44.540
As I rebounded, it happened again, this time sending me off course, towards a garbage can.

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:46.900
A woman caught up with us.

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:51.380
She'd retrieved Tess's baby sunglasses that had fallen to the sidewalk a while back.

00:27:52.150 --> 00:27:53.120
Here, she said.

00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:54.820
You let the baby drop these.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:57.580
Thanks, I said.

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:00.900
You should be careful, she said.

00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:06.000
Telling a blind person he should be careful is like telling him to look out.

00:28:06.980 --> 00:28:09.360
It's not a question of should, but how.

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:14.180
I thanked her again and tried to fit the glasses back onto Tessa's face.

00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:18.700
Mostly, though, I just poked at her chubby cheeks with the arms.

00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:23.560
We shuffled on.

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:27.620
Soon, I recognized a voice at a sidewalk cafe table.

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:36.220
The voice belonged to Joe, an older Italian man who continues to be, as best I can determine, shackled to my preferred coffee shop.

00:28:37.540 --> 00:29:11.240
my god he said as we approached you got a little baby he was up and at us in seconds pinching tess's cheeks this was a man who looked past my blindness and her vulnerability he simply drank in the baby and her babiness it was refreshing i'm telling you joe said coming up for breath this is a hell of a beautiful baby she likes me i can tell he tickled the baby some more And look, Joe went on, she's got her little sunglasses on, and, uh...

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:15.840
Suddenly, all the espresso-fueled joy drained from his body.

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:17.560
The glasses.

00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:21.260
My God, no, he said, his voice low and serious.

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:22.940
She don't see.

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:25.640
My God, she don't see like you.

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:29.800
It took a few minutes to convince him that everything was fine.

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:34.560
He found it hard to believe that babies might wear sunglasses for comfort.

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:51.660
As we rounded the corner at Gravely Street, stepping past the pub and local U-Brew, a mere 30 yards from home plate, I heard the ridiculous girth of an SUV shoot out from a building's underground parking lot.

00:29:53.120 --> 00:30:03.200
The weight of its supersized engineering and Freudian neurosis blew across the sidewalk in front of us, close enough, in fact, to bat the cane from my hand and into the street.

00:30:04.520 --> 00:30:05.100
My heart stopped.

00:30:06.100 --> 00:30:07.540
I didn't know if Tess had been clipped.

00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:09.360
Everything happened so fast.

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:15.440
She sucked wind, readying a hail of tears and a permanent distrust of her father's guidance.

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:17.480
Nothing came, though.

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:19.380
And still nothing came.

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:22.060
So I knew she was shaping that worst cry.

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:24.960
The deep, silent, open-mouthed cry.

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:28.100
The one that can't find any voice in the beginning.

00:30:28.100 --> 00:30:32.420
I braced myself, and then it arrived.

00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:38.280
She violently shook and kicked and squealed, with laughter.

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:46.740
Out of her came a glee powerful enough to start my heart again, a laugh like I've never heard before.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:49.540
Meanwhile, the driver had stopped.

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:51.180
He fetched my cane.

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:59.260
Had I been one step closer home when the SUV had left the lot, My spine would have resembled what now remained of my cane.

00:31:00.920 --> 00:31:04.020
Sorry there, the driver said and handed me my new boomerang.

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:05.680
Didn't see you coming.

00:31:06.540 --> 00:31:06.860
Cute baby.

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:15.000
Before I knew what to say or remembered how to yell, he was back inside his tank, putting it in hyperdrive.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:20.660
You should be careful, he said from out the window, and sped away.

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:55.160
back to daniel burn reading a true story by ryan knighton an excerpt from knighton's book come on papa dispatches from a dad in the dark this story originally aired on the cbc radio program wiretap which is hosted by jonathan goldstein and distributed in the united states by pri public radio international coming up what to do when your neighborhood watch is low rent wise guys we have practical practical tips.

00:31:55.700 --> 00:32:00.000
That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:05.020
This is American Life from Ira Glass.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.780
Each week on our show, of course, we choose a theme, bringing different kinds of stories on that theme.

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:10.660
Today's show, Neighborhood Watch.

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:13.780
Stories of people deciding if they're going to look out for their neighbors or not.

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:16.780
And now we have arrived at Act 3 of our show.

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:19.480
Act 3, Witness for the Pousecution.

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:24.040
A surprising amount of of Debbie Logan's workday involves dogs.

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:31.340
I'm being asked if you want the dog to be intermittently barking in the interview or not, if we have a dog in the office.

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:33.140
Debbie spoke to me from her office.

00:32:33.780 --> 00:32:40.220
She's a building manager for a development of apartments and townhouses in Nashua, New Hampshire called Twin Ponds, 364 units.

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:44.020
And in those 364 units, at least 250 dogs, she says.

00:32:44.940 --> 00:32:49.660
Since they started advertising their dog friendliness about a year ago, business has been unstoppable.

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:50.040
unstoppable.

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:52.400
We have no way to breed restrictions.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:55.820
We have one small dog playground on our property.

00:32:55.960 --> 00:32:58.340
We're in the process of building a large one.

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:07.840
And that's why a lot of people come to us and why our occupancy rate is so high, is 99%. Wow. People love their dogs.

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:10.480
Take that, economic recession.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:13.660
But of course, there's a problem that goes with having lots of dogs around.

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:17.300
And that's human beings who don't pick up after their dogs.

00:33:17.860 --> 00:33:21.820
You see this pretty much everywhere we're in the world where there's large numbers of people and dogs living together.

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:26.370
And here at Twin Ponds, there's a woodsy area and a little brook and, of course, two ponds.

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:30.140
Lots of potential places for wayward, hidden poo.

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:34.400
There was a lot of problems, but it was impossible to find out who they were.

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:39.300
They were very sneaky and they would go out at night or around a corner when nobody's looking.

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:47.840
And the bigger problem was that it was showing up in grassy areas where kids are meant to play.

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:53.380
and around the ponds when people are going over there to enjoy the fishing.

00:33:53.940 --> 00:34:01.980
And one of the biggest things was I'd get a call that said, well, I can't prove it, but I swear it's so-and-so in whatever unit number.

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:03.700
Well, I can't do anything with that.

00:34:04.100 --> 00:34:08.660
She tried a neighborhood watch kind of approach to the problem for a while, put the residents on the lookout.

00:34:09.140 --> 00:34:13.560
We did offer to pay people if they could show us a video of another offender.

00:34:14.640 --> 00:34:19.400
It only worked for a very short amount of time, and we found out very quickly that people aren't comfortable doing that.

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:21.880
Wait, it worked for a brief amount of time?

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:24.640
How many people shot videos of their neighbors?

00:34:25.460 --> 00:34:26.940
It was a while ago.

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:30.720
I would say I maybe had, let's go with half a dozen.

00:34:31.640 --> 00:34:32.140
Half a dozen?

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:36.000
And most of those videos, I couldn't even tell who the person was anyway.

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:38.240
It just wasn't working.

00:34:38.380 --> 00:34:41.440
And the more we thought about that, that wasn't the right way to do things.

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:43.340
So Debbie began researching.

00:34:43.740 --> 00:34:46.020
She spent four or five months looking for some alternative.

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:50.820
looking at what cities do and what dog parks do to get people to pick up after their dogs.

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:53.900
And what she found was that nobody seemed to be doing much at all.

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:57.580
She could put in video cameras everywhere, and that would cost a fortune.

00:34:57.940 --> 00:35:00.040
She didn't want to have to pass that cost on to her tenants.

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:12.060
And finally, poking around on Google, she found a service called PooPrints, which would DNA test all the dogs in her buildings and compare that DNA to DNA found in any errant poo.

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:20.400
Watson and Crick, when they discovered the double helix in a strand of DNA, Crick supposedly announced, we have found the secret of life.

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:23.760
Now Poop Prince was using that to keep the sidewalk clean.

00:35:24.520 --> 00:35:31.620
And I happened to come across it finally and initially I thought that it was a little on the crazy side to DNA dogs.

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:32.160
Really?

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:33.060
Why did it seem crazy?

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:34.740
Doesn't it sound crazy?

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:37.600
Yeah, it actually does sound crazy.

00:35:37.720 --> 00:35:39.080
It just seems like, really?

00:35:39.680 --> 00:35:40.540
Things have come to this?

00:35:40.880 --> 00:35:41.940
That's what we're going to do?

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:43.480
And it's working.

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:44.980
It's working?

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:46.630
though it took some effort.

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:51.960
First step, Debbie had to get samples of all the DNA of all the dogs in all of her buildings.

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:54.300
She had the owners bring the dogs to her office.

00:35:54.920 --> 00:36:00.320
They swabbed the inside of each dog's cheek with a Q-tip, paid $30 to cover the cost, and sent it to the lab.

00:36:00.830 --> 00:36:03.540
Which you would think would be simple, except that they're dogs.

00:36:03.990 --> 00:36:05.640
You have to schedule them one at a time.

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:12.400
You don't want the risk of you're doing a dog DNA in the office and you have someone else coming in, and if those dogs greet each other, then that's it.

00:36:12.760 --> 00:36:13.980
You have to reschedule that appointment.

00:36:14.110 --> 00:36:17.280
You mean because the dogs lick each other.

00:36:17.480 --> 00:36:18.060
That's the problem.

00:36:18.060 --> 00:36:19.500
They're swapping DNA. That's right.

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:23.280
So we only do an appointment every hour.

00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:26.800
And we stayed open late and weekends.

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:40.840
So one of the funnest, the things that I love the most right now, of course, is if somebody calls us and, you know, they've found a pile, quote unquote, on the property, we send a maintenance guy off and we have a collection kit.

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:45.740
and it's, you know, we play a little CSI out there and we get our sample, send it in.

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:54.680
And Trishie says the poor maintenance guy who has to get a dab of poo and then mix it with a special solution and FedEx that sample to the lab in Knoxville, not much fun for him.

00:36:55.540 --> 00:36:57.200
But just how exciting does Debbie find it?

00:36:57.490 --> 00:37:02.080
She finds it so exciting that she has a music cue, a music cue on the ready on her computer.

00:37:02.580 --> 00:37:08.100
So pretty much when I know there's a sample to be picked up, cue up the music and crank it.

00:37:08.220 --> 00:37:13.020
When one of my maintenance guys is walking through the door, He knows exactly what he'll be doing when he hears this.

00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:24.840
Well, I don't think we have as much excitement as Deb does on our end, but...

00:37:25.310 --> 00:37:29.060
And does your poo technician, does he get excited when he actually gets samples to test?

00:37:29.900 --> 00:37:30.300
No.

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:33.960
That's another day at the lab.

00:37:35.140 --> 00:37:39.110
This is Jim Simpson, the president of the company that does the DNA testing called Biopet.

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:43.070
Biopet was the kind of company that ran blood tests for veterinarians.

00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:51.490
Then a few years ago, they realized that since the dog genome had been mapped, there must be a way to make money selling DNA testing of some kind to dog owners.

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:54.320
So they started doing doggy paternity tests.

00:37:54.720 --> 00:37:55.900
Yes, there's a market for that.

00:37:56.500 --> 00:38:03.380
Also, they sell a test that tells you pretty inexpensively exactly what breeds make up your dog, though other companies offer those services as well.

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:11.500
But then somebody at Biopet read about a town in Israel that had a huge problem with dog waste and that hoped to solve it with DNA testing of dogs.

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:19.180
They wondered if it would be possible to get a DNA sample from dog poo and ship it to a lab in usable form.

00:38:20.340 --> 00:38:22.010
This turned out to be kind of a tricky thing to figure out.

00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:24.620
Bacteria grows in the poo sample if you don't do it right.

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:30.420
But finally, about two months ago, they had it down, and they started to market the product, PooPrints, in earnest.

00:38:31.300 --> 00:38:34.860
Only six places have bought it so far, which is why the company president knows Debbie by name.

00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:37.320
She is his biggest customer for PooPrints.

00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:40.400
But Jim Simpson thinks the potential is huge.

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:41.720
We think it's worldwide.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:49.540
We think it's, you know, we've got representatives all over the world that are starting to push the product and they are getting interest.

00:38:49.720 --> 00:38:56.340
But we think it's going to be a material part of our business, you know, at the end of one year.

00:38:57.000 --> 00:39:06.560
Now, I would think that another problem in terms of just getting this product out is that there are lots of places where there might be, you know, dog waste in front of a building.

00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:11.640
But it's in a city or it's on a street where all kinds of dogs come by who don't live in that building.

00:39:11.800 --> 00:39:14.320
And so testing all the dogs in that building isn't going to help you.

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:15.360
That's correct.

00:39:15.520 --> 00:39:18.010
Yeah, it definitely has to be a defined area.

00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:20.480
Most apartment complexes are a gated type community.

00:39:21.780 --> 00:39:25.900
And they have a defined area for the dogs to go to the restroom.

00:39:26.020 --> 00:39:27.160
So it seems to work quite well.

00:39:28.360 --> 00:39:35.100
We do have some small islands that are looking at the program as well.

00:39:36.200 --> 00:39:36.420
That's right.

00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:46.620
he said islands, specifically on the island of Cyprus in the Mediterranean, the town of Limassol, an environmental health official inquired about bringing in poo prints to solve their poo problem.

00:39:47.260 --> 00:39:58.540
Meanwhile, an executive in Copenhagen wrote that perhaps they could collaborate with Biopet and offered to help with politics to institute mandatory adoption of poo prints either all through Denmark or at least in its biggest cities.

00:39:59.820 --> 00:40:05.920
Though it has to be said that when people start If you start debating mandatory DNA dog testing, they don't always end up choosing it.

00:40:06.360 --> 00:40:12.260
I think what ended up happening in our building was, you know, people kind of fell into camps.

00:40:12.880 --> 00:40:19.080
Richard Hopp is a resident of the Scarlet Place Condominiums in Baltimore, a fancy address near the water at the Inner Harbor.

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:31.280
In this spring, a rash of doggy droppings in the hallways, and supposedly even in the elevators of their high-end building, led the condo board to consider DNA testing and $500 fines for violators.

00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:34.480
Richard tipped off a reporter for the Baltimore Sun.

00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:36.700
He was squarely against the testing.

00:40:37.240 --> 00:40:41.830
He says that two puppies were being house -trained in the building at the same time, and that might have been a lot of the problem.

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:43.780
What was the big deal, he asked?

00:40:44.480 --> 00:40:45.720
Why start complicated new rules?

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:57.740
You know, what happened at the very beginning was the board had come up with a set of rules on how, you know, what the fines would be and, you know, that guest dogs, In order to bring a guest dog into the building, they'd have to be DNA tested.

00:40:57.900 --> 00:41:03.340
And the people at the front door were supposed to check every dog that came in the lobby to see if they had a tag.

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:05.660
This very elaborate set of rules.

00:41:06.440 --> 00:41:09.180
Everybody was just kind of laughing about it, and I thought it was just funny.

00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:17.380
So in my mind, I was thinking, first of all, how do you know that you have every dog in the building tested?

00:41:18.500 --> 00:41:20.960
At least my little dogs don't have photo ID.

00:41:21.580 --> 00:41:26.880
So how is anyone going to know if I show up to have my dog tested that it's really sparky?

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:28.620
Who would know?

00:41:29.260 --> 00:41:29.600
Wait a second.

00:41:29.660 --> 00:41:37.240
You're saying that people, members of the building, would actually try to evade the rules by bringing in a random dog for the dog test?

00:41:37.460 --> 00:41:38.100
That's what you're saying?

00:41:38.420 --> 00:41:39.600
Well, I don't know what would happen.

00:41:39.700 --> 00:41:50.380
But if you consider the kind of person you're dealing with is the sort of person who would watch their dog make a number two in the hallway inside the building and not do anything about it.

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:55.840
So if you're dealing with that kind of a person to begin with, then, you know, who knows what they're going to do.

00:41:55.980 --> 00:42:00.460
And it just seems so silly to try and get, you know, a cheek swab from my little dog.

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:05.440
I mean, my dog doesn't even have a snout, so I'm not even sure that he really even has a cheek.

00:42:05.540 --> 00:42:08.060
But I don't even know how you – he's not going to cooperate.

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:13.820
And just the whole thing just seems so overly complicated and overly expensive.

00:42:14.480 --> 00:42:14.960
I don't know.

00:42:15.020 --> 00:42:17.940
It's a Q-tip that you put into his mouth and then you pull it out.

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:18.520
I don't know.

00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:19.800
It doesn't seem so hard.

00:42:20.560 --> 00:42:21.160
I don't know.

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:22.010
I guess I've never tried.

00:42:22.540 --> 00:42:30.320
I suppose it's possible, but it seemed like there was an easier solution, which is either just look at the closed-circuit cameras.

00:42:30.800 --> 00:42:32.380
Are there closed-circuit cameras in the hallways?

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:35.580
Yes. Or deal with it on a neighborly basis.

00:42:36.500 --> 00:42:37.360
It's a condominium building.

00:42:37.740 --> 00:42:41.600
I know, for instance, on my floor that there are four other dogs.

00:42:42.380 --> 00:42:46.900
And if there was a mess, I would be able to pretty well identify where it came from.

00:42:47.220 --> 00:42:55.960
And you just, you know, you talk to people in a neighborly way rather than, you know, trying to use this technological advancement.

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:00.660
The article in the Sun papers got picked up everywhere, all over the country.

00:43:01.120 --> 00:43:04.600
There were TV stories, and this was not good for the building, Richard says.

00:43:05.660 --> 00:43:09.220
Board members who had supported the DNA testing, he says, were made to feel ridiculous.

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:11.360
The DNA idea was dropped.

00:43:12.240 --> 00:43:14.940
The simple threat of action was enough to make the poo problem vanish, too.

00:43:15.680 --> 00:43:18.100
You know, there's still her feelings in the building.

00:43:19.200 --> 00:43:22.580
There's two board members who won't even acknowledge me at this point.

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:27.760
If our paths cross in the lobby, yeah, they were very upset by it.

00:43:34.060 --> 00:43:37.200
The apartment buildings up in Nashville, New Hampshire, are a very different situation.

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:38.830
Lots more dogs.

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:42.520
All the illegal poo is outside, where there are no closed-circuit TV cameras.

00:43:42.910 --> 00:43:47.660
And Debbie Logan says that the simple threat of DNA testing has had a huge effect.

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:57.300
I would say it only took about a month's time to see a dramatic difference once we started going, once the word started getting out there that we were doing this.

00:43:58.340 --> 00:44:01.770
The property has cleaned up 100, you know, not 100%, 99%.

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:07.020
Now, since you've started this, how many times have you had violations that you had to investigate?

00:44:07.120 --> 00:44:07.240
get?

00:44:08.140 --> 00:44:10.680
We have actually fined six pet owners.

00:44:11.620 --> 00:44:20.440
And believe it or not, one of the people was a two-time offender, which is mind-boggling.

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:22.960
It's a pretty steep fine when we catch you.

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:23.660
What's the fine?

00:44:24.120 --> 00:44:24.640
$100.

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:27.940
What did the person say the second time?

00:44:29.660 --> 00:45:04.020
uh the pet owner informed me that they believed they were having problems with another tenant and they thought it was a conspiracy and that it was uh set up it was staged and did they lay out exactly how that would go that somebody would abduct their dog squeeze poo out of it and then and then plant it like did they did they did they lay out a whole scenario um actually the scenario was that they knew where they disposed of their dog droppings and that they must have collected in the middle of the night and then set it up on the property elsewhere, da-da-da.

00:45:04.080 --> 00:45:04.680
It was hilarious.

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:10.280
In the last two decades, DNA evidence introduced an element of certainty to our criminal justice system.

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:12.880
Wrongly convicted men were sprung from prison.

00:45:13.460 --> 00:45:15.520
There was a clarity where things had been cloudy and ambiguous.

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:20.300
Debbie says that it's the certainty that's the most novel thing about doggy DNA testing.

00:45:20.900 --> 00:45:28.480
And it comes at such a cheap price and removes her from the he-said, she-said world of tenant disputes that's the bane of a property manager's existence.

00:45:29.260 --> 00:45:32.060
It's her favorite thing about the doggy DNA program, the certainty.

00:45:33.200 --> 00:45:37.580
If people don't pay their $100 fines within a month, Debbie says that she'll start the eviction process.

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:39.320
She's not kidding around.

00:45:40.040 --> 00:45:40.800
She runs a tight ship.

00:45:41.880 --> 00:45:42.920
But it hasn't come to that yet.

00:45:51.940 --> 00:45:52.380
at.

00:45:52.380 --> 00:45:59.440
Sometimes a neighborhood watch is targeted at watching just one person.

00:46:00.180 --> 00:46:03.220
That happened to Jim O'Grady, and he was not happy about it at the time.

00:46:03.520 --> 00:46:05.740
He told the story of it on stage at the Moth.

00:46:06.220 --> 00:46:17.300
Just a note before we start, we beep out an occasional curse word in this story, and also we we beep out the name of Jim's nemesis, or at least his nemesis for the purposes of this story.

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:19.540
Here's Jim McGrady.

00:46:20.550 --> 00:46:35.200
It's the mid-90s, and I'm living in a beach bungalow, and I look out the window one day, and I see three large men in black suits and shades standing in front of a Lincoln Town car doing this.

00:46:38.320 --> 00:47:05.680
and I know right away that they're there to do one of three things beat me up kill me or more likely make me think that they're there to beat me up or kill me so I flashback right away to the week before where I had bumped into in the local diner and and seeing me had reminded him that the day before I had been on the local news insulting him.

00:47:06.740 --> 00:47:18.480
So he raised his like slicked head from his steak and eggs and he said to me, you should remember that I can have you taken care of.

00:47:20.030 --> 00:47:26.180
And there was something in the way he said taken care of that I knew he didn't mean it tenderly.

00:47:28.010 --> 00:48:26.220
so I'm living at the time beautiful place, tree line lanes my neighbors are retirees and railroad workers and immigrants the problem is we're all in court fighting about whether the residents should stay or go we're fighting for our homes the other problem is that I am very poor at risk assessment so it's my name on the op-ed calling greedy and it is my face on tv calling him an aesthetic cretin i was very proud of that phrase um and that's how come three men are standing outside my window and i try to work i go i'm a writer i go back but i'm going back and forth to the window looking out over hours and as I'm doing this I think three things.

00:48:26.600 --> 00:48:26.920
Two things.

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:29.280
I have a simple thought process.

00:48:30.300 --> 00:48:33.560
One, being a wise guy is f***ing boring.

00:48:34.930 --> 00:48:41.220
The whole time they're standing there listening to the bird chirp and checking out their man manicures.

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:59.820
And the other thing I think is about a guy I'd read about named Clarence Jordan, a white man who had founded an interracial farming commune in rural Georgia in 1942. And this got the attention of the local Ku Klux Klan.

00:49:01.220 --> 00:49:08.060
So every now and then at night they would come by, shoot a shotgun, throw a firebomb at the houses.

00:49:08.390 --> 00:49:51.040
So Clarence had a problem, but Clarence was a disciple of non-violence so he did one thing that you do is you try to find some mundane human activity that you share with your enemy and use it to make them see you as a person so one day he's at the local post office and he sees some clan guys and he goes over and he says hey fellas are you married and they go yeah he says children you have children they go sure he says well you know how out when a baby wakes in the night and it's fussing and you just can't get it to go back to sleep and you're up till dawn and they say, oh, yeah, God.

00:49:52.380 --> 00:50:00.860
And he said, well, when you shoot at us, it wakes the babies.

00:50:02.480 --> 00:50:05.740
And we have a hard time getting him to go back to sleep.

00:50:06.620 --> 00:50:57.160
So I take that thought and I combine it with my complete lack of common sense and instead of calling the cops I go out my front door and I go up to the three guys and I say to the big guy in the middle I say you've been out here for a while now you need the the bathroom and nobody was a muscle and the big guy says now we're good and I think Clarence Jordan what am I now what and I said well are you hungry and the This is a quote.

00:50:57.280 --> 00:50:58.640
This is an exact quote from the big guy.

00:50:58.740 --> 00:51:05.000
He says, well, to be honest with you, I would not say no to a bowl of lentil soup.

00:51:16.140 --> 00:51:17.540
And that's how it starts.

00:51:17.800 --> 00:51:21.500
You know, like 20 minutes later, we're four of us around my little kitchen table.

00:51:22.240 --> 00:51:58.440
and and it turns out they're not even mobsters they're unemployed construction workers they've taken this job from because they're hoping he'll hire them to build the McMansions once he kicks us off the land and raises our houses so it's not the soup is not enough to get them on my side, but it's enough that they tell me is a prick who pays them next to nothing and made them get man manicures because he thought it would make them threatening.

00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:13.160
The end of the story is we made it, we came up with a system for the the afternoons when it got really tedious for these guys.

00:52:14.000 --> 00:52:17.220
One of them would take the car to the end of the lane and be the lookout.

00:52:17.500 --> 00:52:20.960
The other two would like hang on my couch, watch my TV, take a nap.

00:52:22.260 --> 00:52:23.460
I was writing a book.

00:52:23.580 --> 00:52:24.780
I kind of liked their company.

00:52:28.520 --> 00:52:33.660
And this went on for like four or five days until the **** was satisfied that I'd been terrified enough.

00:52:36.200 --> 00:52:41.860
And, you know, I'm not going to exaggerate and say that that me and these guys became friends, because we didn't.

00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:50.380
But I do, I really do, I do think, maybe, maybe I'm, I don't know.

00:52:50.800 --> 00:52:54.380
I think that if after, here's what I think.

00:52:56.920 --> 00:53:01.120
We did share some mundane human activities.

00:53:01.520 --> 00:53:35.220
And I think that if after that first day, after the soup had asked them to harm me I think they would have told him to take his six bucks an hour and shove it up his a** Jim O'Grady is co-author of the biography Disarmed and Dangerous The Radical Lives and Times of Daniel and Philip Berrigan It was recorded at the Moth which features personal stories told live in front of an audience For more Moth stories you can listen to the Moth Radio Hour from PRX.

